course: medicine
lesson: 23
title: Sunshine, vitamin D and water
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sunlight carries invisible ultraviolet (UV) rays: a little UVB on your skin helps it make vitamin D for strong bones, but too much burns the skin and damages it for life.
explain: You never need to burn to make vitamin D: a short time outside in summer is enough, and then it's shirt, sun cream, hat, shade and sunglasses.
explain: Your body is more than half water, and you lose some with every breath, every drop of sweat and every wee, so it needs topping up all day.
explain: Picture a sports day in July: by lunchtime the kids who forgot their bottles have headaches and dark yellow wee, while the ones sipping water all morning are still running.
fact: UVB rays in sunlight help the skin make vitamin D, which helps the body absorb calcium for bones, and you don't need to burn to make it.
fact: Sun cream should be at least SPF 30 with good UVA protection, put on before going out and reapplied often, especially after swimming or sweating.
fact: UV rays can damage the eyes too, so sunglasses should carry a UV-protection mark, and dark lenses alone don't mean better protection.
fact: Clouds don't block all UV rays, so you can burn on a cloudy, windy or cool day; the UV index on weather apps shows how strong the sun is.
fact: Australia's sun campaign began as «Slip, Slop, Slap», shirt, sun cream and hat, later adding «Seek» shade and «Slide» on sunglasses, because Australia has one of the highest skin cancer rates in the world.
fact: Even mild dehydration can cause headaches, tiredness and poor concentration, and you can be dehydrated before you feel thirsty.
fact: UK advice for children is about 6 to 8 drinks a day, more in hot weather or exercise, and milk and watery foods count as well as water.
fact: Pale straw-coloured wee is a sign of drinking enough, and dark yellow wee is a sign the body needs more water; wee as clear as water every single time isn't the goal either.
fact: Heat exhaustion makes people dizzy, sick and sweaty, and needs shade, water and cooling; heatstroke, with confusion, fainting or hot dry skin, is an emergency for 999.
fact: Fizzy drinks have lots of sugar and acid, and energy drinks have caffeine, which can upset sleep and speed up the heart, so health advice says children shouldn't have them; even diet fizzy drinks, with no sugar, are still acidic and wear away teeth.
fact: Many UK shops already refuse to sell energy drinks to under-16s, and a legal ban on such sales in England was announced in 2025.
fact: Being able to drink water in class helps children drink enough and concentrate.
exercise: What does sunlight on skin help the body make? | Vitamin D | Vitamin C | Iron | Fibre
exercise: Do you need to burn to make vitamin D? | No, a short time in the sun is enough | Yes, a little pink is needed | Yes, a proper deep tan is needed every summer | Only in winter
exercise: What sun cream is recommended for children? | SPF 30 or more with UVA protection | Any cream at all, once a day | SPF 5 for darker skin | Any body lotion with a nice strong smell
exercise: When should you put sun cream on again? | Often, and after swimming or sweating | Never, one coat lasts all day | Only when you already feel hot and sticky | Only at the end of the day
exercise: What should good sunglasses have? | A UV-protection mark | Very dark lenses, whatever the mark | A mirror coating | Bright coloured frames
exercise: Can you get sunburnt on a cloudy day? | Yes, clouds don't block all UV | No, the clouds block all of the UV | Only if it's hot and sunny as well | Only at the seaside, near the water
exercise: What do the three words Slip, Slop, Slap stand for? | Shirt, sun cream, hat | Sandals, sunglasses, shade | Swim, sleep, splash | Salad, soup, apple
exercise: Why did Australia start its sun campaign? | It has one of the highest skin cancer rates | Its sun is a different colour | It had too many beaches | Its people never went outside
exercise: What can even mild dehydration cause? | Headaches and poor concentration | Stronger bones and healthier teeth | Much better night vision in the dark | Lots of extra energy for running about
exercise: About how many drinks a day do UK children need? | About 6 to 8, more when hot | About 2, whatever the weather | About 20 big glasses | None if they eat fruit
exercise: What does dark yellow wee tell you? | You need to drink more | You are very healthy | You ate carrots | You drank too much water
exercise: What should happen when someone feels dizzy and sick in the heat? | Move into shade, drink water and cool down | Run around to warm up | Wrap up in a coat | Drink an energy drink
exercise: Which sign makes heat illness a 999 emergency? | Confusion or fainting | Feeling rather thirsty | Sweating a bit on the face | Wanting an ice lolly to cool down
exercise: Why shouldn't children have energy drinks? | Caffeine can upset sleep and heart rate | They have no taste | They make you too clever | They contain vitamins that children need every day
exercise: Why are diet fizzy drinks still a problem for teeth? | They are acidic | They are full of sugar | They have caffeine in all of them | They are too cold
exercise: Why do schools let children keep water bottles in class? | Drinking enough helps them concentrate | To stop them going to the canteen at lunch | To water the plants | To make them go home early
test: "cloudy innit, no need for suncream" — true? | No, UV gets through clouds | Yes, clouds stop all the sun | Yes, if it's cold enough out | No, but only if you're in Spain
test: Your mate puts sun cream on at 9 and swims all afternoon. What's wrong? | It needs reapplying, especially after swimming | Nothing, one coat lasts | He should have used less | Swimming in the pool adds more protection to the cream
test: Kyle got a sunburn "to make loads of vitamin D". Right? | No, burning is damage and you don't need it for vitamin D | Yes, more burn, more vitamin D | Yes, burns make bones stronger | No, vitamin D only comes from pills
test: Dad buys cheap very dark sunglasses with no UV mark. Problem? | Dark lenses without UV protection may not protect the eyes | No problem, darker is always better | They'll make eyesight sharper | They only matter in winter
test: "I'm not thirsty so I'm not dehydrated" — after 2 hrs footy in July. Hmm? | You can be dehydrated before you feel thirsty | Right, no thirst means no problem at all, so keep playing | Thirst only matters in winter | Only adults get dehydrated
test: What would happen if a child drank only fizzy drinks all day? | Lots of sugar and acid would harm teeth | Teeth would get stronger | Nothing, fizzy drinks are really just water | Vitamin D would rise
test: Grandpa's sitting in the sun, confused, red and not making sense. What now? | Call 999, it could be heatstroke | Give him a strong cup of hot coffee | Let him sleep it off there in the sun | Wrap him up in a warm, thick blanket
test: Which has the strongest sun risk? | A clear midday in July with a high UV index | A cloudy, windy evening in the middle of December | A rainy morning in November | Night time
test: My mate's wee is like water every time, is he sick? | Pale yellow is fine, clear every time isn't the goal | Yes, he has a disease | Yes, he needs fizzy drinks | No, it means he needs more water
test: "Slip Slop Slap is for Aussies, we're in Manchester". Right? | Sun safety matters everywhere, UV can burn in Britain too | Right, UK sun can't burn | Right, it's only for beaches | Only if you live near the sea
test: In a game your hero drinks an energy can for +speed. What's true for kids in real life? | Energy drinks aren't advised for children | They really make kids run faster and jump higher | They're like water | They're good for sleep
exam: Determine the condition of the urine which is customarily regarded as indicative of adequate hydration. | Pale straw colour | Dark amber colour | Complete absence of colour on every occasion | Bright orange colour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning ultraviolet radiation on overcast days. | Cloud cover does not intercept all ultraviolet radiation, and sunburn may occur | Cloud cover intercepts all ultraviolet radiation | Ultraviolet radiation is emitted solely in hot weather | Sunburn occurs only at the coast
exam: Whereas a person exposed to heat exhibits confusion and loss of consciousness, identify the appropriate course of action. | Summoning emergency services by telephoning 999 | Administration of a strong and caffeinated beverage | Continued exposure until spontaneous recovery | Covering the person with heavy blankets
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the consumption of energy drinks by children is discouraged. | Their caffeine content may disturb sleep and heart rate | They contain no water whatsoever | They are injurious solely to adults | They impede the synthesis of vitamin D
